; expect 130
; MAC-HYG-8's FIRST hole, closed (2026-08-16). This file is what the
; diagnostics fixture `590-macro-capture` was - deleted with its three
; goldens - measuring the ANSWER where that measured the refusal.
;
; A template's free identifier means what it meant WHERE THE MACRO WAS
; WRITTEN. For a module macro `MAC-HYG-7` enforces that by rewriting
; the name to `Mod$name`, which nothing at a call site can shadow, and
; `364-macro-definition-site.ax` pins it. An ENTRY-FILE macro had
; nothing to qualify to - the entry file's declarations are left bare,
; deliberately - so a binding of the same name at the invocation
; captured the reference and the program computed with the local. This
; file's `shadowed` answered 0 where the macro's own `helper` answers
; 40, at exit 0, with no diagnostic from any pass; then `AX3032`
; refused it, which was loud but rejected a correct program.
;
; WHAT CLOSED IT is not what the specification predicted. `MAC-HYG-9`
; says the mechanism must become scope sets - an identifier as a
; `(name, scopes)` pair - and that is a change to what an identifier
; IS. What this case actually needs is one bit of that: a macro is a
; TOP-LEVEL declaration, so a template's free identifiers had no
; enclosing local scope where they were written, and every one of them
; means something top level. `substTpl` stamps them (`setNodeDefScope`,
; node word 10) and both resolvers skip the local scope for a stamped
; reference - `checkVar`, and `emitVar` through `dispatchCall`.
;
; Both, because they disagreed at first: with only the checker taught,
; this file type-checked against the macro's `helper` and the emitter
; still called the local, so `shadowed` answered 0 while a version
; whose local was an `Int` checked clean. A checker typing one binding
; while the emitter reads another is the shape worth naming.
;
; Four claims, and the weights are 1, 2, 32 and 95:
;
;    1  a `let` binding of the template's free name at the invocation
;       does not capture it: 40, not the local's 0. This is the hole
;    2  TWO invocations under one such binding each resolve to the
;       macro's own `helper`: 10 + 20 = 30, not the local's 1 + 1.
;       So the fix is per REFERENCE and not per macro
;   32  the same macro where NOTHING shadows it still answers 40 -
;       the case that always worked and must keep working
;   95  this file's own reference to its own `helper` still means its
;       own: `(helper 9)` is 90. Hygiene runs in both directions, and
;       a rewrite that sent every bare name to the top level would
;       break nothing here - but a template binder is not a free
;       identifier, and `372-decl-macro.ax` is the fixture that says
;       so: a generated function's PARAMETERS reach the same arm and
;       must not be stamped. Measured - they were, and 372 became
;       `AX3001 undefined variable x`
;
; 1 + 2 + 32 + 95 = 130. The compiler before this commit refuses this
; file with three `AX3032`s; the one before THAT answers 122, which is
; this total with the two captured terms reading 0.
(macro (useHelper v) (helper v))

(:: helper (-> Int Int))

(fn (helper x) (* x 10))

(:: shadowed Int)

(fn (shadowed)
  (let ((helper 
    (lambda (y) 0)
  ))
    (useHelper 4)
  )
)

(:: shadowedTwice Int)

(fn (shadowedTwice)
  (let ((helper 
    (lambda (y) 1)
  ))
    (+ (useHelper 1) (useHelper 2))
  )
)

(:: safe Int)

(fn (safe) (useHelper 4))

(:: b2i (-> Bool Int))

(fn (b2i x)
  (if x
    1
    0
  )
)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) (+ (b2i (== shadowed 40)) (+ (* 2 (b2i (== shadowedTwice 30))) (+ (* 32 (b2i (== safe 40))) (* 95 (b2i (== (helper 9) 90)))))))
